\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $k$ be algebraically closed and let $A$ be a finitely generated $k$-domain of dimension $d>2$. If adjoining $m>0$ polynomial variables gives $A[t_1,\ldots,t_m]\cong k[x_1,\ldots,x_{m+d}]$, must $A$ already be a polynomial ring in $d$ variables over $k$?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2007/01}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $k$ be algebraically closed and let $A$ be a finitely generated $k$-domain of dimension $d>2$. If adjoining $m>0$ polynomial variables gives $A[t_1,\ldots,t_m]\cong k[x_1,\ldots,x_{m+d}]$, must $A$ already be a polynomial ring in $d$ variables over $k$?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30000650 (OWR-1452-014). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2007/01}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30000650}

