\section*{Higher-Dimensional Cancellation for Affine Domains}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
An explicit complex affine fourfold is claimed to become affine five-space after one stabilization while itself failing to be affine four-space, with stable-coordinate and nontrivial affine-fibration consequences. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 047, 2026-09-23): A locally nilpotent derivation and an explicit inverse polynomial change give the cylinder isomorphism. To obstruct cancellation, a valuation along the exceptional parameter computes the associated graded affine modification; the resulting rigidity distinguishes the original coordinate ring from a polynomial algebra.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 047, 2026-09-23): The explicit finite-type complex domain has d=4 and A[w] is the polynomial ring in five variables while A is not polynomial. This is exactly a permitted algebraically closed-field example with m=1 and d>2, so it disproves the universal cancellation implication.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 047, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected construction is a four-dimensional complex domain whose one-variable polynomial extension is a five-variable polynomial ring, while the original ring is not polynomial. The other affine-fibration and stable-coordinate consequences are separate. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: ComplexCancellation.lean. Declarations: OAI.ComplexCancellation.main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

